no registry asked: the planes are as the enrollment claims, and no-remote-actions denies the convention's write leaf zensight/v1/*/@rpc/*/**/set rather than the declared write procedures — pass --registry <dir> to narrow both to what the fleet actually declares (RFC 13 §3 O4)

```

`--json5` is the deliverable — zenohd's block, field for field zenoh 1.10
(`zenoh-config-1.10.0/src/lib.rs`), with a comment per rule naming the
matrix row it instantiates and the fact it exists for. A foreign schema, so
`--format` has no say over it.

```console
$ zenctl acl gen --enrollment tests/cmd/fixtures/enrollment.toml --json5
// zenohd access_control block — generated by `zenctl acl gen` (RFC 09 §3).
// base "zensight"; 6 subject(s), 20 rule(s), 6 polic(y/ies); registry: not asked — planes are as the enrollment claims and the write set is the convention's `set` leaf, unnarrowed.
// Field names: zenoh 1.10 (zenoh-config-1.10.0/src/lib.rs — AclConfig, AclConfigRule,
// AclMessage, InterceptorFlow, AclConfigSubjects, AclConfigPolicyEntry). A rule with no
// `flows` applies in both directions. Merge at the router config's top level; the
// three lists are all required — rules alone are refused at startup (fact 3). ACL
// config is not runtime-reloadable: enrolling a host is a router restart (RFC 03 §4 D6).
// ! write_set_not_narrowed: no registry asked: the planes are as the enrollment claims, and no-remote-actions denies the convention's write leaf zensight/v1/*/@rpc/*/**/set rather than the declared write procedures — pass --registry <dir> to narrow both to what the fleet actually declares (RFC 13 §3 O4)
access_control: {
  enabled: true,
  default_permission: "deny",  // fact 4: deny, and every declaration allowed by name
  rules: [
    // host-data: RFC 09 §3 fact 1: the data classes and the alive tokens — and nothing under @rpc, @media or @blob, which `**` never reaches
    { id: "host-data-h-3fa9c2d41b7e", permission: "allow", flows: ["ingress"],
      messages: ["put", "delete", "liveliness_token"],
      key_exprs: ["zensight/v1/h-3fa9c2d41b7e/**"] },
    // host-serve: RFC 09 §3 facts 1 and 4: @rpc spelled explicitly because `**` will not cross it; declare_queryable allowed or the host serves nothing; query egress is the router forwarding calls to it
    { id: "host-serve-h-3fa9c2d41b7e", permission: "allow",
      messages: ["declare_queryable", "reply", "query"],
      key_exprs: ["zensight/v1/h-3fa9c2d41b7e/@rpc/**", "zensight/v1/h-3fa9c2d41b7e/@blob/**"] },
    // host-media: RFC 09 §3 fact 1: the plane needs its own rule
    { id: "host-media-h-3fa9c2d41b7e", permission: "allow", flows: ["ingress"],
      messages: ["put"],
      key_exprs: ["zensight/v1/h-3fa9c2d41b7e/@media/**"] },
    // host-blob-seed: RFC 09 §3, RFC 07 §2: the sanctioned one-shot PUT path into the router content store — omit in deployments without one
    { id: "host-blob-seed-h-3fa9c2d41b7e", permission: "allow", flows: ["ingress"],
      messages: ["put"],
      key_exprs: ["zensight/v1/h-3fa9c2d41b7e/@blob/store/**", "zensight/v1/h-3fa9c2d41b7e/@blob/tree/**"] },
    // host-adv: RFC 09 §3, RFC 04 §3.3: the sidecars live under a verbatim @adv suffix host-data's `**` cannot reach — omitting this when the tier is in use fails silently: empty seeds, dead recovery
    { id: "host-adv-h-3fa9c2d41b7e", permission: "allow",
      messages: ["put", "liveliness_token", "declare_queryable", "reply", "query"],
      key_exprs: ["zensight/v1/h-3fa9c2d41b7e/**/@adv/**"] },
    // interest-prop: the fifth fact (reference deployment 2026-08-30): zenoh checks a consumer's declares and queries on egress toward the responding face against that face's subject, and own-origin grants include no wildcard-origin selector — without this a peer-mode publisher publishes to nobody. Egress-only, declares and queries only: puts and replies stay ingress-checked
    { id: "interest-prop", permission: "allow", flows: ["egress"],
      messages: ["declare_subscriber", "declare_liveliness_subscriber", "liveliness_query", "query"],
      key_exprs: ["zensight/v1/**", "zensight/v1/@catalog/**", "zensight/v1/*/@rpc/**", "zensight/v1/@catalog/@rpc/**", "zensight/v1/*/@blob/**", "zensight/v1/*/@media/**", "zensight/v1/**/@adv/**", "zensight/v1/@catalog/**/@adv/**"] },
    // host-data: RFC 09 §3 fact 1: the data classes and the alive tokens — and nothing under @rpc, @media or @blob, which `**` never reaches
    { id: "host-data-h-20609002f7b6", permission: "allow", flows: ["ingress"],
      messages: ["put", "delete", "liveliness_token"],
      key_exprs: ["zensight/v1/h-20609002f7b6/**"] },
    // host-serve: RFC 09 §3 facts 1 and 4: @rpc spelled explicitly because `**` will not cross it; declare_queryable allowed or the host serves nothing; query egress is the router forwarding calls to it
    { id: "host-serve-h-20609002f7b6", permission: "allow",
      messages: ["declare_queryable", "reply", "query"],
      key_exprs: ["zensight/v1/h-20609002f7b6/@rpc/**", "zensight/v1/h-20609002f7b6/@blob/**"] },
    // host-data: RFC 09 §3 fact 1: the data classes and the alive tokens — and nothing under @rpc, @media or @blob, which `**` never reaches
    { id: "host-data-h-0123456789ab", permission: "allow", flows: ["ingress"],
      messages: ["put", "delete", "liveliness_token"],
      key_exprs: ["zensight/v1/h-0123456789ab/**"] },
    // host-serve: RFC 09 §3 facts 1 and 4: @rpc spelled explicitly because `**` will not cross it; declare_queryable allowed or the host serves nothing; query egress is the router forwarding calls to it
    { id: "host-serve-h-0123456789ab", permission: "allow",
      messages: ["declare_queryable", "reply", "query"],
      key_exprs: ["zensight/v1/h-0123456789ab/@rpc/**", "zensight/v1/h-0123456789ab/@blob/**"] },
    // host-adv: RFC 09 §3, RFC 04 §3.3: the sidecars live under a verbatim @adv suffix host-data's `**` cannot reach — omitting this when the tier is in use fails silently: empty seeds, dead recovery
    { id: "host-adv-h-0123456789ab", permission: "allow",
      messages: ["put", "liveliness_token", "declare_queryable", "reply", "query"],
      key_exprs: ["zensight/v1/h-0123456789ab/**/@adv/**"] },
    // catalog-own: RFC 09 §3 fact 2: `*` never covers a verbatim origin, so the catalog's own keys are their own rule — @rpc and, on the advanced tier, @adv spelled beside them (fact 1)
    { id: "catalog-own-catalog", permission: "allow",
      messages: ["put", "delete", "liveliness_token", "declare_queryable", "reply", "query"],
      key_exprs: ["zensight/v1/@catalog/**", "zensight/v1/@catalog/@rpc/**", "zensight/v1/@catalog/**/@adv/**"] },
    // catalog-intake-declare: RFC 09 §3 fact 4: the catalog DECLARES interest on ingress; split by flow from the receive half so it cannot ingress-publish any host's keys
    { id: "catalog-intake-declare-catalog", permission: "allow", flows: ["ingress"],
      messages: ["declare_subscriber", "declare_liveliness_subscriber", "liveliness_query", "query"],
      key_exprs: ["zensight/v1/**", "zensight/v1/**/@adv/**"] },
    // catalog-intake-recv: RFC 09 §3: the catalog RECEIVES data and tokens on egress — a flowless rule here would let it tombstone any host's keys, defeating the per-host enrollment
    { id: "catalog-intake-recv-catalog", permission: "allow", flows: ["egress"],
      messages: ["put", "delete", "reply", "liveliness_token"],
      key_exprs: ["zensight/v1/**", "zensight/v1/**/@adv/**"] },
    // ops-sub: RFC 09 §3 fact 4: every plane named, because a declare that is not allowed receives nothing and `**` reaches no verbatim plane (fact 1)
    { id: "ops-sub", permission: "allow", flows: ["ingress"],
      messages: ["declare_subscriber", "declare_liveliness_subscriber", "liveliness_query", "query"],
      key_exprs: ["zensight/v1/**", "zensight/v1/@catalog/**", "zensight/v1/*/@rpc/**", "zensight/v1/@catalog/@rpc/**", "zensight/v1/*/@blob/**", "zensight/v1/*/@media/**", "zensight/v1/**/@adv/**", "zensight/v1/@catalog/**/@adv/**"] },
    // ops-recv: RFC 09 §3: what the console receives, on egress, over the same planes
    { id: "ops-recv", permission: "allow", flows: ["egress"],
      messages: ["put", "delete", "reply", "liveliness_token"],
      key_exprs: ["zensight/v1/**", "zensight/v1/@catalog/**", "zensight/v1/*/@rpc/**", "zensight/v1/@catalog/@rpc/**", "zensight/v1/*/@blob/**", "zensight/v1/*/@media/**", "zensight/v1/**/@adv/**", "zensight/v1/@catalog/**/@adv/**"] },
    // ops-own-token: RFC 09 §3: the console's own subscriber-detection token, confined to @adv — a broad ingress liveliness_token allow would let it forge any host's state/*/alive roster entry
    { id: "ops-own-token", permission: "allow", flows: ["ingress"],
      messages: ["liveliness_token"],
      key_exprs: ["zensight/v1/**/@adv/**", "zensight/v1/@catalog/**/@adv/**"] },
    // no-remote-actions: RFC 09 §3: the write procedures are deniable per key because the key IS the target; deny wins, and is sound under default-deny
    { id: "no-remote-actions", permission: "deny",
      messages: ["query"],
      key_exprs: ["zensight/v1/*/@rpc/*/**/set"] },
    // watch-sub: RFC 09 §3 fact 4, for a read-only observer: the data classes, the catalog and RPC reads — never @media, never @blob
    { id: "watch-sub", permission: "allow", flows: ["ingress"],
      messages: ["declare_subscriber", "declare_liveliness_subscriber", "liveliness_query", "query"],
      key_exprs: ["zensight/v1/**", "zensight/v1/@catalog/**", "zensight/v1/*/@rpc/**", "zensight/v1/@catalog/@rpc/**", "zensight/v1/**/@adv/**", "zensight/v1/@catalog/**/@adv/**"] },
    // watch-recv: RFC 09 §3: what a read-only observer receives, on egress
    { id: "watch-recv", permission: "allow", flows: ["egress"],
      messages: ["put", "delete", "reply", "liveliness_token"],
      key_exprs: ["zensight/v1/**", "zensight/v1/@catalog/**", "zensight/v1/*/@rpc/**", "zensight/v1/@catalog/@rpc/**", "zensight/v1/**/@adv/**", "zensight/v1/@catalog/**/@adv/**"] },
  ],
  subjects: [  // the enrollment: transport identity ↔ origin (RFC 03 §4 D6)
    { id: "h-3fa9c2d41b7e", cert_common_names: ["h-3fa9c2d41b7e"] },  // host
    { id: "edge-02.example", cert_common_names: ["edge-02.example"] },  // host
    { id: "h-0123456789ab", cert_common_names: ["h-0123456789ab"] },  // host
    { id: "zensight-catalog", cert_common_names: ["zensight-catalog"] },  // catalog
    { id: "zensight-console", cert_common_names: ["zensight-console"] },  // console
    { id: "zensight-watch", cert_common_names: ["zensight-watch"] },  // watch
  ],
  policies: [
    { id: "h-3fa9c2d41b7e", rules: ["host-data-h-3fa9c2d41b7e", "host-serve-h-3fa9c2d41b7e", "host-media-h-3fa9c2d41b7e", "host-blob-seed-h-3fa9c2d41b7e", "host-adv-h-3fa9c2d41b7e", "interest-prop"],
      subjects: ["h-3fa9c2d41b7e"] },
    { id: "edge-02.example", rules: ["host-data-h-20609002f7b6", "host-serve-h-20609002f7b6", "interest-prop"],
      subjects: ["edge-02.example"] },
    { id: "h-0123456789ab", rules: ["host-data-h-0123456789ab", "host-serve-h-0123456789ab", "host-adv-h-0123456789ab", "interest-prop"],
      subjects: ["h-0123456789ab"] },
    { id: "zensight-catalog", rules: ["catalog-own-catalog", "catalog-intake-declare-catalog", "catalog-intake-recv-catalog", "interest-prop"],
      subjects: ["zensight-catalog"] },
    { id: "zensight-console", rules: ["ops-sub", "ops-recv", "ops-own-token", "no-remote-actions"],
      subjects: ["zensight-console"] },
    { id: "zensight-watch", rules: ["watch-sub", "watch-recv", "no-remote-actions"],
      subjects: ["zensight-watch"] },
  ],
}
no registry asked: the planes are as the enrollment claims, and no-remote-actions denies the convention's write leaf zensight/v1/*/@rpc/*/**/set rather than the declared write procedures — pass --registry <dir> to narrow both to what the fleet actually declares (RFC 13 §3 O4)

```

```console
$ zenctl acl gen --enrollment tests/cmd/fixtures/enrollment.toml --json5 --format json
? 2
error: the argument '--json5' cannot be used with '--format <FORMAT>'

--json5 emits a foreign document format — somebody else's schema — while --format chooses among zenctl's own three renderings of a report. Drop one.

Usage: zenctl <COMMAND>

For more information, try '--help'.

```

## Narrowed by a registry

With `--registry`, the planes are what host producers actually declare and
`no-remote-actions` denies exactly the `kind = "write"` procedures — the
fixture registry's `systemd/action/set`, the blob `artifact/request`
requests, the catalog's `link`/`unlink` — rather than the convention's
`set` leaf. The dead bus keeps the case offline; the dirs answer alone.

```console
$ zenctl acl gen --enrollment tests/cmd/fixtures/enrollment.toml --format ndjson --registry ../fixture-tests/registry --zenoh-config tests/cmd/fixtures/dead-bus.json5
no session (failed to open the Zenoh session); answering from --registry only: ../fixture-tests/registry.
That is what this checkout declares, not what the fleet serves — `zenctl doctor --registry <dir>` compares them when the bus is reachable (RFC 05 §3.1).
